# Intel AJI client adapter

This port adds an `aji_client` adapter that connects OpenOCD to the Intel
Quartus JTAG server (`jtagd` on Linux or `jtagserver.exe` on Windows). It
supports physical TAPs and Intel SLD nodes exposed as OpenOCD virtual TAPs.

## Build

Run the normal OpenOCD bootstrap process and enable the adapter explicitly:

```sh
./bootstrap
./configure --enable-aji-client
make
```

On Windows the adapter loads `libaji_client.dll` at runtime. Place that DLL
beside `openocd.exe`; it can be built from the Intel `libaji_client` source.

On Linux, `libaji_client` is linked into OpenOCD. If it is not installed in a
standard library directory, pass its location and dependencies explicitly:

```sh
LDFLAGS="-L/path/to/libaji_client" \
AJI_CLIENT_LIBS="-laji_client -lstdc++ -ldl -lpthread" \
./configure --enable-aji-client
```

`AJI_CLIENT_LIBS` can be overridden for distributions of the library that
need additional dependencies.

## Configuration

Select the adapter as usual:

```tcl
adapter driver aji_client
```

When more than one cable is visible, select one by the name reported by the
Quartus JTAG tools:

```tcl
aji_client hardware cable0 {USB-BlasterII}
```

Declare an SLD node after declaring its physical parent TAP:

```tcl
jtag newtap fpga tap -irlen 10 -expected-id 0x02e050dd
vjtag create fpga.tap.niosv \
    -chain-position fpga.tap \
    -expected-id 0x08986e00
target create fpga.niosv riscv -chain-position fpga.tap.niosv
```

See `tcl/board/altera_arria10_niosv__aji_client.cfg` for a complete example.

The optional `QUARTUS_JTAG_CLIENT_CONFIG` environment variable is passed to
the Quartus AJI runtime in the same way as other AJI clients.
